The greatest in heaven
At that time the disciples came to Jesus and asked, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called for a child and put him in their midst.
He said to them, "Truly I tell you, unless you change and become like little children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes like this child is greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
And before that, I accepted one like this in my name.
And it would be better for him to have a millstone hung around his neck and to be drowned in the sea, than that he should offend one of these little ones who believe in me.
Woe to the world because of causes of sin! Such things must come, but woe to that man by whom the cause comes!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away; it is better for you to enter life maimed or crippled than with two hands or two feet to be thrown into eternal fire.
If your eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and throw it away from you; for it is better for you that one of your members perish than for your whole body to be thrown into hell. It is like the lost sheep.
Look, don't despise these little ones, for I tell you that their angels in heaven always see my Father who is in heaven.
For the Son of Man came to seek and save what was lost.
What do you think? If someone has one hundred sheep and loses one of them, won't they leave the ninety-nine in the mountains and go look for the lost one?
And if he finds it, truly I say to you, he rejoices more over it than over the ninety-nine that did not go astray.
This is my Father's will that none of these little ones should perish. If your brother sins against you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him in private and reproach him. If he listens to you, then you have gained your brother.
And if he does not listen, take one or two others with you, so that every word may be confirmed by the testimony of two or three men.
If they won't listen to you, tell it to the church. And if they don't listen to the church either, treat them like pagans and tax collectors.
I tell you this: Whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And I tell you also, that if two of you agree on earth about anything you ask for, it will be done by my Father who is in heaven.
For where two or three gather in my name, there am I with them." Like the slave who does not forgive
Then Peter came to Jesus and asked, "Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother or sister who sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus said to him, "Not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
Therefore, heaven's kingdom is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
When he started to settle his accounts, one debtor came up with ten thousand pounds in debt.
And if he has nothing to pay his master's debt with, then both he and his wife and children will be sold along with all that belongs to him.
The slave was proud and bowed down to him saying: Oh master, give me time so I can pay you back everything.
Then that master freed his slave and let him go with all his debts paid off.
When that slave went out, he found one of his fellow slaves who owed him a hundred denarii; so he seized him and began to choke him, saying, 'Pay what you owe me.'
The slave's pride was his companion at his feet and he asked him saying: "Wait for me so I can pay you back everything."
He didn't respond but went ahead and threw him in jail until he paid his debt.
When their fellow slaves saw what had happened to him, they were deeply saddened. They came and told everything that had happened to their master.
Then his master called him in and said to him, "You wicked servant! I forgave you your entire debt because you begged me."
Shouldn't you have shown mercy to your fellow slave just like I showed mercy to you?
His master was furious and handed him over to the jailers until he could pay back everything he owed.
That's how your heavenly Father treats you if you don't forgive each other from your hearts."
Marriage and divorce
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went to Judea across the Jordan.
And many people followed him there, and he healed them all.
The Pharisees came to test him, saying, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?"
He replied to them, "Haven't you read that at the beginning God created them male and female?"
And he said: For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife, and they will become one flesh.
"Therefore what God has joined together, let man not separate."
They said to him: "So why did Moses order that a divorce document be given so she could get divorced?"
He said to them, "Because of your hard hearts Moses allowed you to divorce your wives, but from the beginning it was not so."
I tell you that anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman commits adultery; and whoever marries a divorced woman also commits adultery.”
His disciples said to him, "If that is so, it is better not to marry."
He said to them: "Not everyone accepts this message but those who have been given it."
For there are eunuchs who were born that way from their mother's womb; and there are eunuchs who were made eunuchs by men; and there are eunuchs who have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. He who is able to accept it, let him accept it." Jesus blesses the children.
Then they brought little children to him that he might touch them; but the disciples rebuked those who brought them.
But Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for to such belongs the kingdom of heaven."
He put his hands on them and went away. The rich young man
And when someone came up to him and said, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to get eternal life?"
He said to him, "Why do you call me good? No one is good except God alone. But if you want to enter life, keep the commandments."
He said to him, "Which commandments?" And Jesus replied, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not bear false witness."
"Honor your father and mother, and love your neighbor as yourself."
The young man said to him: "I have learned all this since I was a child. What else do I need?"
Jesus said to him, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this he went away sad because he had many possessions.
Jesus said to his disciples, "Truly I tell you, it is hard for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of heaven!"
And I tell you this too: it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.”
When his disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
But Jesus looked at them and said to them, "With men this is impossible, but with God all things are possible."
Then Peter answered him, saying, "We have left everything to follow you! What then will there be for us?"
Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, at the renewal, when the Son of Man sits on his glorious throne, you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
And everyone who has left houses or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or fields for my name's sake will receive a hundredfold and inherit eternal life.
But many who think they're first will be last, and those who think they're last will be first.
Like an ass in the vineyard
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who was a householder and went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard.
I agreed with the workers to pay them one dinar per day, and sent them to my vineyard.
Then he went out around three o'clock and saw other idlers standing about in the market-place.
He said to them, "Go work in my vineyard too for what is right." So they went.
He also went out about six o'clock and nine o'clock and did likewise.
Then about eleven o'clock he got up and found some others standing there idle. He said to them, "Why are you here all day long doing nothing?"
They said to him, "Because we didn't hire you." He replied, "Go work in someone else's vineyard then."
When evening came, the innkeeper said to his servant: "Call the workers and pay them their wages, starting with those who were hired last and going on to those who were hired first."
Then came those who were hired about eleven o'clock, and each received a denarius.
When the first ones came, they thought they would get more. So they also received one denarius each.
While they were taking it, they complained about the owner of the house.
They said: "These others worked one hour, but they've been paid the same amount we who have borne the burden of the day and the heat!"
He replied to one of them: "My friend, I haven't wronged you! Didn't we agree on a dinar?"
Take what belongs to you and go; for I want to give this last one just as much as you.
Or is it forbidden for me to do what I want with my own? Or is your eye evil because I am righteous?
"This is why last will be first and first will be last." Jesus foretells his death and resurrection.
As Jesus was going up to Jerusalem, he took the twelve disciples aside along the way and said to them:
We're going up to Jerusalem, where the Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and scribes, who will condemn him to death.
They will hand him over to the Gentiles to be mocked and flogged and crucified, and he will rise again on the third day.” The mother of my son Zebedee
Then my mother-in-law Zebda came to him with her two daughters, prostrated herself before him, and asked for something from him.
He asked her, "What do you want?" She said to him, "Let these two sons of mine sit one at your right hand and the other at your left in Your kingdom."
Jesus replied, "Do you know what you're asking? Can you drink from my cup or be baptized with the baptism I am to receive?" They said to him, "We can."
He said to them, "My cup you will drink it, and with the baptism I am baptized with you will be baptized; but sitting at my right hand or at my left is not mine to give, but it is for those for whom it has been prepared by my Father."
When they heard this, the ten brothers were angry with them.
Jesus called them together and said, "You know that those who are regarded as rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their high officials exercise authority over them."
It should not be like that among you. But whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant.
Whoever wants to be first among you must serve everyone else.
"For even the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many." Healing two blind men in Jericho
As they were leaving Jericho, a large crowd followed them.
And two blind men were sitting by the roadside. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they shouted, "Have mercy on us, Lord, Son of David!"
So he rebuked them sharply for being so noisy; but they shouted all the louder, "Have mercy on us, Master; Son of David!"
Jesus stood up and called them over to him. He asked, "What do you want me to do for you?"
They said to him, "Master, let our eyes be opened!"
Then Jesus touched their eyes, saying, "According to your faith be it unto you." And immediately their eyes received sight, and they followed him.
Entering Jerusalem
And when they drew near to Jerusalem, and came to Bethphage at the mount of Olives, then sent Jesus two disciples, saying unto them,
He said to them, "Go into the village that is in front of you, and there you will find an ass tied up, which has been set apart for me; untie it and bring it here to me."
And if anyone says anything to you both, say: "The Lord needs them." And then he'll send him away right away.
So all this was done to fulfill what was said by the prophet who said:
Tell the daughter of Zion: Behold your king is coming to you, meek, riding on an ass, even on a colt, the foal of an ass.
The two disciples went and did exactly what Jesus had told them to do.
They brought two donkeys and put their clothes on them, then sat down on them.
And some others spread their clothes in the road; and others cut down branches from the trees and strewed them in the way.
The crowds that went ahead and those who followed were shouting, saying: "Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!"
When he entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up, saying, "Who is this?"
The crowd said, "This is Jesus the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Purifying the Temple
Jesus went into the temple courts and drove out all who were buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the benches of those selling doves.
He said to them, "It is written: My house will be called a house of prayer; but you have made it 'a den of robbers.'"
And he healed many who were sick with various diseases, and cast out many demons.
When the chief priests and scribes saw the miracles he had done, and heard the children crying in the temple saying, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" they were indignant.
They said to him, "Do you hear what these people are saying?" But Jesus replied, "Yes, I have heard it; haven't you read this passage from Scripture? 'From the lips of children and infants you, Lord, have called forth your praise.'"
Then he left them and went outside the city to Bethany, where he spent the night. The fig tree withered.
In the morning when he was returning to the city, he became hungry.
He looked at a fig tree beside the road, and finding no figs thereat, said to it, "May you never bear fruit again!" And when even now he still did not find what he was looking for, he left the way and walked across the fields.
When the disciples saw this they were amazed and asked, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?"
Jesus replied, "Truly I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, not only can you do what was done to the mulberry tree, but also you can say to this mountain, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' and it will be done."
"And whatever you ask in prayer, believing, you will receive." The question is about Jesus' authority
When he came to the temple courts, the chief priests and elders approached him while he was teaching and said, "By what authority do you do these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered them, "I will also ask you one question; then answer me and I will tell you by what authority I do these things:
John's baptism: where was it from? From heaven or men?" They discussed among themselves saying: "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will reply, 'Then why didn't you believe him?'"
If we say: "from among people," we fear the masses because John is considered like a prophet to everyone."
They answered Jesus, "We don't know." So he said to them, "Neither am I telling you by what authority I do this. The parable of the two sons"
What do you think? A man had two sons, so he went to his first son and said: "Go work in my vineyard today."
He answered: "Nothing." But he changed his mind later and went away.
He came to the second one and said likewise. And he answered and said, "Here I am, Lord." But he did not go.
"Which one did what his father wanted?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly I tell you, the tax collectors and the prostitutes are entering the kingdom of God ahead of you."
For John came to you in the way of righteousness, but you did not believe him; whereas the tax collectors and harlots believed him. And when you saw it, you did not even repent afterward so that you might believe him. Like the children of this age
Listen to another parable: A man was a landowner who planted a vineyard, put a wall around it, dug a winepress in it, built a watchtower, leased it to some tenants, and went away.
When it was time for the fruit to ripen, he sent his servants to the tenants so they could gather his fruit.
The Karamites took their slaves, some were whipped, others killed, and still others stoned to death.
Then he sent other slaves who were more numerous than the first two, and they did likewise to them.
Finally he sent his son to them with this message: "They should fear my son!"
But when they saw the son, they said to each other, "This is the heir! Come, let's kill him and take his inheritance!"
They took him outside the vineyard and killed him.
When the master comes, what will he do to those servants?
They said to him: "Those crafty ones will be destroyed miserably, and their land will pass to other noblemen who will give it its fruits in due season."
Jesus said to them, "Haven't you read in the Scriptures: 'The stone that the builders rejected has become the cornerstone; this is from the Lord, and it is marvelous in our eyes!'?"
That's why I tell you that God's kingdom will be taken away from you and given to a people who produce its fruit.
"Whoever falls upon this stone will be crushed to dust, but whoever is struck by it will be shattered!"
When the chief priests and Pharisees heard this, they knew he was talking about them.
And when they were trying to arrest him, he was afraid of the crowd because they regarded him as a prophet.
Like a royal wedding
And Jesus spoke to them in parables, saying:
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who made a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to call those who had been invited to the wedding feast, but they wouldn't come.
He also sent other servants to say, 'Tell those who have been invited, "Look, I've prepared my dinner; my oxen and fattened cattle have been slaughtered, everything is ready. Come to the wedding banquet!"'
But they were careless and went their separate ways - one to his field, another to his business.
The rest captured their slaves, beat them up, and killed them.
When the king heard this, he was furious and sent his soldiers to kill those murderers and burn their city.
Then he said to his servants: "The wedding is ready, but those who were invited weren't worthy."
Go to the street corners and invite everyone you find to the wedding feast.
So those slaves went out onto the roads and gathered together all whom they found, both good and bad; and so the wedding hall was filled with guests.
When the king came in to look at the guests, he saw a man who was not wearing wedding clothes.
He said to him, "Friend, how did you get in here without wedding clothes?" But he was silent.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie him hand and foot, take him away, and throw him into the outer darkness, where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth."
"Because many claim but few get elected." Paying taxes to Caesar
Then the Pharisees went away and plotted how to trap him in what he said.
So they sent their disciples to him along with some Herodians, saying, "Teacher, we know that you're honest and teach God's way in line with truth. You don't care about anyone because you don't look at people's faces."
So tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?”
But Jesus knew their thoughts and said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Show me what you pay in tribute." They handed him a dinar.
He said to them: "Whose picture is this?"
They said to him, "Give to Caesar what is Caesar's." He replied, "Give to Caesar what belongs to Caesar, but give to God what belongs to God."
When they heard this, they were amazed and left him alone. The question about the resurrection of the dead
On that day some Sadducees came to him, who say there is no resurrection; and they asked him,
They said: "O teacher! Moses said that if someone dies without children, his brother should marry his wife and have children for him."
We had seven brothers, the first one got married but died. Since he didn't have children, his wife was given to his brother.
And likewise the second and third up to the seventh.
And in the end, the woman died too.
In the resurrection, which one of the seven will be your wife? Because she was for all of them!
Jesus replied, "You don't know the Scriptures or the power of God."
For in the resurrection they neither marry nor are given in marriage but are like angels in heaven.
As for the resurrection of the dead, haven't you read what God said to you before?
"Am I God's son?"
When they heard his teaching, the crowd was amazed at what he said. The greatest commandment
But when the Pharisees heard that he had silenced the Sadducees, they came together.
One of them, who was a mosquito, asked him to test it by saying:
Teacher, which is the greatest commandment in the law?
فقال له يسوع: "تحب الرب إلهك بكل قلبك وبكل نفسك وبكل عقلك."
This is the first and greatest commandment.
And the second is like it: Love your neighbor as yourself.
"These two commandments include all laws and prophets." Jesus and David
While the Pharisees were gathered together, Jesus asked them
He said to them, "What do you think about Christ? Whose son is he?" They replied, "The son of David."
He said to them, "How can David then call him 'Lord,' saying:
The Lord said to my Lord: "Sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies a footstool for Your feet."
If David called him Lord, how could his son be anything less?
No one could answer him; from that day forth no one dared to ask him anything more.
Warning against the scribes and Pharisees
Then Jesus spoke to the crowds and his disciples.
Saying: "The scribes and Pharisees sat on Moses' seat."
Whatever they tell you to do, do it, but don't follow their example because they say one thing and do another.
They load people down with burdens hard to carry, but they themselves won't lift a finger to move them.
And they do all their works to be seen by men; therefore they have enlarged their phylacteries and made broad their borders.
They love to sit at the head table in feasts and take the first seats in meetings.
And greetings in the markets, and people calling them: "My master!"
And don't call anyone your master here on earth, because you have one Master, and you are all brothers.
And do not call anyone your father here on earth, for you have one Father in heaven.
And do not call anyone teacher, because you have one Teacher, who is Christ.
The greatest among you will be your servant.
Whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted. Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites!
But woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you shut up the kingdom of heaven against men; for you neither go in yourselves, nor do you allow those who are entering to go in.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you devour widows' houses, and for a pretense make long prayers. Therefore you will receive greater condemnation.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You travel over land and sea to win a single convert, and when he becomes one, you make him twice as much a son of hell as you are.
Woe to you, blind guides! You say, "Whoever swears by the temple is bound by nothing; but whoever swears by the gold of the temple is bound."
Oh you fools and blind people! Which is greater: gold or the temple that sanctifies gold?
And whoever swears by an altar is nothing; but he who swears by the offering that is upon it shall be bound to keep his oath.
Oh you fools and blind men! Which is greater: the offering or the altar that sanctifies the offering?
Whoever swears by an altar has sworn by it and everything upon it!
And whoever swears by Al-Kaaba has sworn by it and its inhabitants.
Whoever swears by heaven has sworn by Allah's throne and the One who sits upon it.
Woe to you, teachers of the law and Pharisees, you hypocrites! You give a tenth of your spices—mint, dill and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law—justice, mercy and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter, without neglecting the former.
O blind leaders! You strain at gnats but swallow camels.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you clean the outside of the cup and plate, but inside they are full of greed and self-indulgence.
O blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and dish so that their outside may also be clean.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you are like whitewashed tombs which outwardly appear beautiful, but within are full of dead people's bones and all uncleanness.
That's how it is with you too; from outside you look good to people but inside you're full of hypocrisy and sin.
Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! For you build tombs for the prophets and decorate the graves of the righteous,
And you say: If we had been with our fathers in that time, we would have certainly been among those who disbelieve.
So you testify against yourselves that you are children of those who killed the prophets.
Fill up the measure of your fathers.
O generation of vipers, how can ye escape the damnation of hell?
That's why I am sending you prophets and wise men and scribes—some of them you will kill and crucify; others you will flog in your synagogues and pursue from town to town.
So that all the righteous blood shed on earth will come upon you, from the blood of Abel the righteous to the blood of Zechariah son of Berechiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.
I have to tell you that all this is coming upon this generation! Jesus weeps over Jerusalem
Oh Jerusalem, oh Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you, how often have I longed to gather your children together, as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings, but you were unwilling!
Behold, your house is left unto you desolate.
I tell you this: You will never see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!"
Signs of the End Times
Then Jesus went out from the temple area and was going away when his disciples came up to him in order to show him the buildings of the temple complex.
Jesus said to them, "Do you see all these things? Truly I tell you, not one stone here will be left on another; every one will be thrown down."
While he was sitting on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to him privately saying, "Tell us when will this happen? And what is the sign of your coming and the end of the age?"
But Jesus answered them, "Watch out that no one deceives you."
For many will come in my name saying I am Christ; and they will deceive many.
And you will hear about wars and rumors of wars. See that you are not alarmed; for this must take place, but the end is not yet.
Because nation will rise against nation, kingdom against kingdom; there will be famines, pestilences, and earthquakes in various places.
But all these are just the beginning of sorrows.
Then they will hand you over to be persecuted and killed, and all nations will hate you because of me.
And then many will stumble and fall over each other, hating one another.
Many false prophets will appear and deceive many people.
And because of their many sins, they lose the love of many people.
But whoever stands firm to the end will be saved.
Wickers bears witness to this gospel of the kingdom throughout all creation for all nations. Then comes the end.
When did you see "the abomination that causes desolation" standing in the holy place? Let the reader understand.
Then those in Judea must flee to the mountains.
And whoever is on the roof should not come down to take anything from his house.
And he who is in the field must not turn back to get his clothes.
Woe to those who are pregnant and nursing in those days!
So that your flight is neither in winter nor on Sabbath.
For then there will be great tribulation such as has not been since the beginning of the world until this time, no, nor ever shall be.
If those days were not shortened, no one would survive. But for the sake of the elect, those days will be shortened.
Then if anyone says to you, 'Look, here is the Christ!' or 'There he is!' do not believe it.
For false messiahs will arise and give great signs and wonders so as to lead astray, if possible, even the elect.
Here I am, ahead of you all.
If they say to you: "Here it is in the wilderness!" do not go forth; if they say: "Here it is in the hiding places!" do not believe them.
For just as lightning comes from the east and is seen even in the west, so will be the coming of the Son of Man.
Where there is a carcass, there will the vultures be gathered together.
And when those days come to an end, the sun will be darkened, the moon won't give its light, the stars will fall from heaven, and the powers in the heavens will be shaken.
And then shall appear the sign of the Son of man in heaven: and then shall all the tribes of the earth mourn, and they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds of heaven with power and great glory.
He will send his angels with a great trumpet blast, and they will gather his elect from the four winds, from one end of the heavens to the other.
From the fig tree learn its lesson: when its branch becomes tender and puts forth its leaves, you know that summer is near.
So also you, when you see these things happening, know that it is near, at the doors.
I have to tell you that this generation won't pass away until all these things take place.
The heavens and earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away. Always be prepared.
But about that day or hour no one knows, neither the angels in heaven, nor the Son; but only the Father.
As it was in Noah's days, so will be also the coming of the Son of Man.
For they were eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day that Noah entered the ark; and they did not know until the flood came and took them all away, so will also the coming of the Son of Man be.
They did not know until the flood came and took them all away. And so will be the coming of the Son of Man.
Then two will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left.
Two women grind at the millstone; one is taken away while the other remains.
Stay awake, for you do not know when your Lord will come.
And know this: If the master of the house knew at what hour the thief was coming, he would keep watch and not let his house be broken into.
Therefore you also be ready, for the Son of Man is coming at an hour you do not expect. Like a faithful servant
Who is this faithful and wise servant whom his master has put in charge of all his possessions to give them their food at the proper time?
Blessed is that servant whom his master will find doing so when he comes.
I have to tell you that he's worth all his money.
But if that wicked slave says in his heart, "My master is taking long to come,"
He starts beating his fellow slaves, eating and drinking with drunks.
The master will come when you least expect him and at an hour you know nothing about.
He will cut him in two and give his portion to the dogs. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Like the ten virgins
Then shall the kingdom of heaven be likened unto ten virgins, which took their lamps and went forth to meet the bridegroom.
And five of them were wise, and five were foolish.
But the foolish ones took their lamps, but they did not take oil with them.
But the wise ones took oil in their lamps along with their own.
As the groom spoke slowly, they all fell asleep one by one.
At midnight there was a cry, "Here's the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all those virgins got up and trimmed their lamps.
The foolish women said to the wise ones: Give us some of your oil because our lamps are going out.
The wise women answered, "It is not enough for us; go rather to the merchants and buy some more."
As they were going to buy some things, the bridegroom came. Those who were ready went in with him to the wedding banquet, and the door was shut.
Finally, the other bridesmaids also came and said, "Lord, Lord, open up for us!"
He answered and said to them, "Truly I say to you, I do not know you."
Stay awake then; you don't know when your master is coming, whether in the evening or at midnight, or when the cock crows, or in the morning. Otherwise he may find you asleep when he comes suddenly.
It's like a traveler who called his slaves to him and gave them his money.
He gave one five talents, another two, and another one; to each according to his ability. Then he went away.
So he who had received five talents went away at once and traded with them, and gained five more talents.
And so he who had received two talents gained two more also.
But the one who took the talent went off and dug in the ground and hid his master's money.
After a long time had passed, the master of those slaves came back to settle their accounts.
Then he who had received five talents came forward, bringing five more, saying, 'Master, you delivered to me five talents; here I have made five more.'
His master said to him: "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master."
Then came he that had received two pounds, and said, Lord, thou deliveredst me two pounds: behold, I have gained two other pounds beside them.
His master said to him: "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful over a little; I will set you over much. Enter into the joy of your master."
Then came also that one who had received the one talent, saying, Lord, I knew you to be a hard man, reaping where you did not sow, and gathering where you scattered no seed.
I was afraid and I went away and hid your weight in the earth. Here is what belongs to you.
His master replied to him, "You wicked and lazy servant! So you knew that I harvest where I haven't sown and gather from where I haven't scattered?"
So you should have put my money with the bankers, so that when I came back, I would get what was mine plus interest.
Take away from him the pound and give it to him who has ten pounds.
For whoever has will be given more, and they will have an abundance. Whoever does not have, even what little they have will be taken from them.
And those who were slaves to sin will be thrown into outer darkness where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. The sheep and goats
And when the Son of Man comes in his glory, with all the angels with him, then he will sit on his glorious throne.
And all nations will be gathered before him; he will separate one people from another like a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats.
He will set the sheep on his right hand, but the goats on the left.
Then the king will say to those on his right, 'Come, you who are blessed by my Father; take your inheritance, the kingdom prepared for you since the creation of the world.'
Because I was hungry you fed me. Because I was thirsty you gave me water to drink. I was a stranger and you welcomed me in.
Naked you found me. Sick you visited me. Imprisoned you came to me.
The righteous will come to Him that Day saying: "O Lord! When did we see You hungry and feed You or thirsty and give You drink?"
When did we see you as a stranger and welcome you in, or naked and clothe you?
When did we ever see you sick or in prison so that we could visit you?
The king replied to them, "I tell you the truth, whatever you did for one of these brothers of mine, you did it for me."
Then he will say to those on his left, "Depart from me, you cursed, into the eternal fire prepared for the devil and his angels."
Because I was hungry but you didn't feed me. I was thirsty but you didn't give me water to drink.
I was a stranger but you didn't shelter me. I was naked but you didn't clothe me. I was sick and imprisoned but you didn't visit me.
Then they will reply to him, saying: Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty or homeless or naked or sick or in prison without serving you?
He replied to them, "Truly I tell you, whatever you did not do for one of these least ones, you did not do for me."
"These will go away to eternal punishment, but the righteous to eternal life."
Plotting to kill Jesus
When Jesus had finished saying these things to his disciples, he said:
You know that in two days it will be Passover, and the Son of Man will be handed over to be crucified.
Then all the chief priests and elders of the people came together to the palace of Caiaphas, who was the high priest.
They plotted to arrest Jesus by trickery and kill him.
But they said, "It's not right to take it from the temple treasury, for that would be against the law." Then he poured the perfume on Jesus.
While Jesus was in Bethany at Simon's house, who had been healed from leprosy
A woman approached him with an expensive perfume bottle and poured it over his head while he was leaning back.
When his disciples saw this they were indignant and said to him, "Why waste it like that?"
"Because this ointment could be sold for much more and given to the poor."
Jesus said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? She has done a good thing for me."
For you have the poor with you always, but me you do not have always.
She poured this perfume over my body to keep me from being buried.
I must tell you that wherever this gospel is preached in the whole world, they will also speak about what was done to her in memory of her." - Judas' betrayal
Then one of the twelve, who was called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests.
He said to them, "What do you want me to give you in exchange for him?" So they paid him thirty pieces of silver.
And from that time he asked for an opportunity to deliver him. The Passover supper with his disciples
On the first day of Unleavened Bread, when it was customary to sacrifice the Passover lamb, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
He said: "Go to the city, to so-and-so, and tell him: The teacher says: My time is near. Can you make the sacrifice with my students?"
The disciples did what Jesus had told them to do and prepared for Passover.
And when evening came it was already past twelve.
As they were eating, he said, "I tell you the truth, one of you will betray me."
They were all deeply saddened and each one asked him: "Am I it?"
He who dips his hand in the dish with me will betray me.
But woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed! It would have been better for him if he had not been born."
Judas answered him, "Is it I, Master?" He said to him, "You have said so." The Lord's Supper
While they were eating, Jesus took bread, blessed it, broke it, and gave it to his disciples saying, "Take this and eat it; for this is my body."
And he took the cup, gave thanks and offered it to them, saying, "Drink from it, all of you."
For this is my blood of the new covenant, which is shed for many for forgiveness of sins.
I tell you this: From now on I will never drink wine with fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new with you in my Father's kingdom."
Then they went swimming and headed to the Mount of Olives. Jesus foretold Peter's denial of him
Then Jesus said to them, "All of you will fall away because of me this night, for it is written: 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.'"
But I will go ahead of you to Galilee."
Peter answered him, "Even if everyone else doubts you, I will never doubt."
Jesus said to him, "Truly I tell you, this very night, before the rooster crows twice, you will deny me three times."
Peter said to him, "Even if I have to die with you, I will never deny you!" And all the disciples said the same thing. In Gethsemane
Then Jesus came with them to a place called Gethsemane, and said to his disciples, "Sit here while I go yonder and pray."
Then he took Peter and James son of Zebedee with him, and began to be distressed and troubled.
He said to them, "My soul is deeply troubled even unto death; abide ye here, and watch with me."
Then he stepped forward a little way and fell to his knees; and he prayed, saying, "Father, if thou art willing, remove this cup from me; nevertheless not my will, but thine, be done."
Then he came to his disciples and found them sleeping. And he said to Peter, "Could you men not keep watch with me for one hour?"
Stay awake and pray so that you will not fall into temptation. The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak.”
So he went away again and prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is not possible for this cup to be taken away unless I drink it, may your will be done."
Then he came to them again, but they were sleeping, for their eyes were heavy.
He left them too, and went away; but he came back again and said exactly the same thing for the third time.
Then he came to his disciples and said to them, "Are you still sleeping and resting? Look, the hour is near, and the Son of Man is delivered up into the hands of sinners."
Let's go! The one who is betraying me is close at hand!" - Jesus' arrest
And while he was speaking, behold, Judas, one of the twelve, came up to him with a great crowd with swords and clubs from the chief priests and elders of the people.
And he who delivered him up said to them, "Whom you want I will deliver up to you; but that you may know that I have power to release him."
Then he came up to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Master!" And he kissed him.
Jesus said to him, "Friend, why have you come?" Then they came up and laid hands on Jesus and took Him.
And one of those who were with Jesus drew his sword and struck the slave of the high priest, cutting off his ear.
Jesus said to him, "Put your sword back in its place; for all who take the sword will perish by the sword!"
Do you think I can't now ask my father to send me more than twelve legions of angels?
How do you complete your books? Is it like this?
At that hour Jesus said to the crowds, "Why do you come with swords and clubs as though I were a criminal? Every day I was among you teaching in the temple courts, and you did not arrest me."
And all this was so that what had been spoken by the prophets might be fulfilled." Then all the disciples deserted him and fled. Before the Sanhedrin
And those who had arrested Jesus took him to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and elders were assembled.
But Peter followed him from afar to the high priest's courtyard, went inside and sat down with the guards to see what would happen.
The chief priests, elders, and entire council were looking for false testimony against Jesus to put him to death.
They couldn't find anyone. And even though there were many false witnesses who came forward, they still couldn't find anything. But finally, two false witnesses stepped up
They said, "This one says that I can tear down God's temple, and in three days rebuild it."
The high priest stood up and said to him, "Why don't you answer anything? What do these two testify against you?"
But Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest said to him, "I put you under oath by the living God to tell us if you are the Christ, the Son of God."
Jesus said to him, "You have spoken correctly; for also the Son of Man is going to sit on the right hand of the Power, and he will come with the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes. "He has spoken blasphemy!" he said. "Why do we need more witnesses?" You have now heard the blasphemy.
What do you see? They replied, "He deserves death."
Then they spat in his face and hit him with their fists; others slapped him.
They said to him, "Tell us then, who hit you?" Peter's denial
But Peter was sitting outside in the courtyard, and a servant girl came up to him and said, "You also were with Jesus of Galilee."
He denied it in front of everyone, saying: "I don't know what you're talking about!"
Then when he went out to the porch, another woman saw him and said to those there, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth!"
I also denied it by swearing: "I don't know that man!"
And then someone came up to Peter and said, "Surely you're one of them too; your accent gives you away!"
He began to swear at him roundly, "I don't know this fellow!" And then the cock crowed.
Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him: "Before the rooster crows today, you will deny three times that you even know me." And he went outside and wept bitterly.
Judas hanged himself
When morning came, all the chief priests and elders of the people conferred against Jesus to put him to death.
They tied him up and took him to Pontius Pilate, the governor.
When Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus was condemned, he changed his mind and brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and elders.
He said: "I have made a mistake in delivering an innocent man to death." They replied: "What do we care? You know better!"
He threw down his silver in the temple, then went away and hanged himself.
The chief priests took the silver coins and said, "It is not lawful to put them into the treasury, since they are blood money."
They went to see it and bought the field from the owner for silver coins; and there that field was called Akeldama, that is, Field of Blood.
That's why that field is still called "The Field of Blood" to this day.
Then was fulfilled what had been spoken by Jeremiah the prophet: "And they took the thirty pieces of silver, the price of him who was valued, whom they priced from among the children of Israel."
And they gave it to me for the potter's field, just as the Lord commanded me." In front of Pilate
Jesus stood before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "You say so."
While the chief priests and elders were complaining about him, he did not answer anything.
Then Pilate said to him, "Do you not hear how many things they testify against you?"
He didn't answer even one word, which surprised the governor greatly.
The governor had made it his custom to release one prisoner at each festival, whoever they wanted.
And at that time they had a notorious prisoner called Barabbas.
When they were gathered together, Pilate said to them, "Which one do you want me to release for you? Barabbas or Jesus who is called Christ?"
Because he knew they had handed him over out of envy.
While he was sitting in his chair of state, his wife sent him word saying: "Mind that fellow there; I've had such an awful dream about him."
But the chief priests and elders stirred up the crowd to ask for Barabbas instead and have Jesus put to death.
The governor said to them, "Which one do you want me to release for you? Barabbas or Jesus?" But they shouted back, "Barabbas!"
Then Pilate said to them, "What shall I do then with Jesus who is called Christ?" They all said together, "Let him be crucified!"
The governor said, "What evil has he done?" But they shouted all the louder, saying, "Crucify him!"
When Pilate saw that he was gaining nothing but causing trouble instead, he took water and washed his hands in front of the crowd. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he said. "It is your responsibility!"
The whole people answered and said, "His blood be upon us and our children."
Then they released Barabbas to them; but Jesus they scourged and delivered him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked him.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace and called together all his troops.
They undressed him and put him in a red robe.
They wove a crown of thorns and put it on his head, along with a reed in his right hand. They knelt down before him and mocked him by saying, "Hail, King of the Jews!"
They spat at him, took a stick and beat his head with it.
After they mocked him, they stripped off his robe and put his own clothes back on him, then led him away to be crucified. Crucifixion
As they were going out, they found a man from Cyrene, named Simon; they forced him to carry his cross.
And when they came to a place called Golgotha (which means Place of a Skull),
They gave him some bitter-tasting stuff to drink, but when he'd tasted it, he didn't want to drink anymore.
When they crucified him, they divided up his clothes by casting lots for them, just as it is written in the scripture: "They divided my garments among themselves and cast lots for my clothing."
Then they sat there guarding him.
They placed an inscription above him that read: "This is Jesus, King of the Jews."
Then two thieves were crucified with him; one on his right hand, and another on his left.
And those who had crossed over were rowing him while shaking their heads.
They said, "If you're God's son, come down from the cross!"
And so too were the chief priests mocking him among themselves with the scribes and elders, saying:
He can save others but cannot save himself! If he is indeed the King of Israel, let him come down now from the cross so that we may believe in him!
He has put his trust in God; let him deliver him now if he wants to. For he said, 'I am God's son.'
And so also those who were crucified with Him reviled Him.
And there was darkness over all the land until the ninth hour.
And at about three in the afternoon Jesus cried with a loud voice, saying, Eli, Eli, lama sabacthani? that is to say, My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?
Some of those standing there said, "He is calling for Elijah."
One of them ran to get a sponge, filled it with water, and gave him some water through a straw.
But the others said, "Let him go; let's see if Elijah comes to save him."
Then Jesus cried out again in a loud voice and gave up his spirit.
And when the curtain in the temple was torn in two from top to bottom, the earth shook, and the rocks were split.
And the tombs were opened; and many bodies of the saints who had fallen asleep were raised.
They came out of the tombs after his resurrection and entered the holy city, appearing to many.
But the captain and those who were with him guarding Jesus saw what had happened to the earthquake and became terrified, saying, "Surely this was the Son of God!"
And there were also many women looking on from afar, who had followed Jesus from Galilee, ministering to Him.
Among them were Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and the mother of Zebedee's sons. The burial
And when evening came, a rich man from Ramah named Joseph came; he too was a disciple of Jesus.
So he went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Then Pilate ordered that it be given him.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in clean linen.
He put him in his new tomb that he had cut out of the rock, then rolled a big stone against the entrance to the tomb and went away.
And there was Mary Magdalene and another Mary sitting opposite the tomb. The guards were at the tomb.
The next day after the preparation, the chief priests and Pharisees gathered at Pilate's place
They said to him: "Sir, you remember that this impostor declared while he was still alive: 'In three days I shall rise again.'"
But he was careful to have the tomb guarded until the third day, so that his disciples wouldn't come at night and steal him away and then say to the people: "He has risen from the dead!" In this way, the last deception would be worse than the first one!
Then Pilate said to them, "You have guards; go yourselves and take him away."
So they went and secured the tomb with guards and sealed up the stone.
The Apocalypse
After Saturday, at dawn on the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary came to look at the tomb.
And when there was a great earthquake, for an angel of the Lord descended from heaven and came and rolled back the stone and sat upon it.
He looked like lightning, and his clothes were white as snow.
The guards trembled in fear and became like dead men.
The angel answered them, "Do not be afraid; you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He is not here; for he has risen, just as he said. Come, see the place where they laid him.
Go quickly, tell his disciples that he has risen from the dead; and behold, he is going before you to Galilee; there you will see him. See, I have told you."
They ran quickly away from the tomb, both filled with fear yet overflowing with joy. They rushed to tell his disciples.
As they were going to tell his disciples what had happened, Jesus met them and said, "Peace be with you." They came up to him, took hold of his feet, and worshiped him.
Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid; go tell my brothers to leave for Galilee; there they will see me." The words of the guards
As they were going there, some guards came to the city and reported everything that had happened to the chief priests.
They met with the elders, consulted together, and gave the soldiers much silver
They say his disciples came at night and stole him while we were sleeping.
And if he hears about it from the governor, we'll beg him to make you feel at ease."
They took the silver coins and did what they were told to do, and this saying has been circulating among the Jews until today. Jesus appears to his disciples
But the eleven disciples went to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go.
When they saw him, they bowed down to worship him, but some doubted.
Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
And teach them to observe everything that I have commanded you. And behold, I am with you all days until the end of the age.” Amen.
John the Baptist paves the way
The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
As it is written in the prophets: "Behold, I am sending my messenger ahead of you; he will prepare your way before you."
A voice crying in the wilderness: "Prepare ye the way of the Lord, make straight his paths."
John was baptizing in the wilderness and proclaiming a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins.
All the people of Judea and all the people of Jerusalem came out to him and were baptized by him in the Jordan River, confessing their sins.
John wore camel's hair and had a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
He was preaching, saying: "One mightier than I is coming after me; I am not fit to stoop down and loosen the thongs of his sandals."
"I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Baptism of Jesus Christ and his temptation
And in those days Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee and was baptized by John in the Jordan.
And when he came up out of the water, immediately he saw the heavens opened and the Spirit like a dove descending upon him.
And there was a voice from heaven saying, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased."
And at that time the spirit will take him to the wilderness.
And he was in the wilderness for forty days being tempted by Satan. And with wild beasts; and angels were ministering to him. The call of the first disciples
After John was put in prison, Jesus went to Galilee preaching the good news about God's kingdom.
And he says: "The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God has come near; repent, and believe in the gospel."
As he was walking by Lake Galilee, Jesus saw two brothers, Simon and Andrew his brother, throwing their nets in the lake because they were fishermen.
Jesus said to them, "Come follow me, and I will make you fishers of men."
They left their windows open and followed him.
Then he went from there a little way, and sat down by the sea. And they hired two ships; in these men had gone fishing that night but caught nothing.
He called them to him and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men and went off after Him. He drove out an unclean spirit.
Then they entered Capernaum, and immediately on the Sabbath He got into the synagogue and began to teach.
They were amazed at his teaching because he taught them like someone who had authority, not just like a scribe.
There was a man in their community who had an unclean spirit, so he cried out
He said to him, "What have you to do with me, Jesus, son of God? Have you come to torment us before our time?"
Then Jesus rebuked him, saying, "Be quiet! Come out of him!"
The unclean spirit threw him down and came out with a loud cry.
They were all confused, even asking each other, "What is this? What new teaching is this? For he commands with authority, even over impure spirits, and they obey him!"
His fame spread throughout all that region of Galilee, and people brought to him all who were ill.
When they left the synagogue, they went straight to Simon and Andrew's house with James and John.
Simon's mother-in-law was sick in bed with a fever, so they told him about her right away.
He came forward holding her hand, and she was cured at once; then she began to serve them.
When evening came, after sunset, they brought to him all who were sick and those who had demons.
The whole city was gathered at the door.
He healed many who were sick with various diseases, cast out many demons, and did not allow the demons to speak because they knew him. Preaching in Galilee
في الصبح بدري اوي قام وخرج ومشي لمكان فاضي، وكان بيصلي هناك
So Simon followed him with those who were with him.
When they found him, they said to him, "Everyone is looking for you."
He said to them, "Let's go to the nearby villages so that I can preach there too, because that is why I came."
He preached in their synagogues throughout Galilee and drove out demons. He healed those with leprosy.
A leper came to him and begged him on his knees, saying, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Then Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying, "I want to; be clean!"
As he was speaking, leprosy left him and he was cured.
So he rebuked him and sent him away for a while.
And he said to him, "Look, don't tell anyone anything, but go offer your sacrifice for purification just as Moses commanded, so that they may be purified."
And he went out and began to shout loudly and spread the news, until he could no longer enter the city openly, but was outside in deserted places, and they came to him from all directions.
Healing paralyzed
Then he went to Capernaum also after some days, and it was heard that he was in a house.
And soon many people gathered around him so that there was hardly room for them all even outside the door. He spoke to them in words.
They brought to him a paralyzed man, carried by four men.
And since they couldn't get close to him because of the crowd, they uncovered the roof where he was lying. And after digging through it, they lowered the bed that the paralyzed man was lying on.
When Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralyzed man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
And some scribes were sitting there thinking in their hearts:
Why does this one speak like that with oars? Who can forgive sins except God alone?
Jesus knew in his spirit that they were thinking this way about themselves, so he said to them, "Why do you think these things in your hearts?"
Which is easier to say to a paralyzed man: "Your sins are forgiven," or "Get up, take your bed and walk"?
But that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins”—and he said to the paralytic—
I say to you, get up, pick up your mat and go home!
He got up at night and carried his bed outside in front of everyone, so they were all amazed and praised God saying: "We have never seen anything like this before!" - The call of Levi
Then he went out again to the sea. And all the crowd gathered around him and he taught them.
As he was walking by, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at his tax booth, and said to him, "Follow me." And he got up and followed him.
While he was reclining at home, many tax collectors and sinners were also reclining with Jesus and his disciples, because they were many in number and followed him.
But when the scribes and Pharisees saw him eating with tax collectors and sinners, they said to his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
When Jesus heard this, he said to them, "Healthy people don't need a doctor—sick people do. I have come to call sinners, not righteous people, to repentance." Question about fasting
John's disciples and the Pharisees were fasting, so they came to Jesus and asked him, "Why do John's disciples and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples don't?"
Jesus said to them, "Can you make wedding guests fast while the groom is with them? As long as they have the groom with them, they cannot fast."
But there will come days when the bridegroom is taken away from them; then they will fast in those days.
No one sews a new patch onto an old garment; otherwise, the new material will eat away at the old fabric, making it even worse.
"No one pours new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the new wine will burst the skins; both the wine and the wineskins will be ruined. Instead, new wine must be poured into new wineskins." - The Lord of the Sabbath
On Saturday he passed through the fields of grain; his disciples began to pick heads of wheat as they went along.
The Pharisees said to him, "Why do they break the Sabbath?"
He said to them, "Haven't you ever read what David did when he was in need and hungry, along with those who were with him?"
How did he enter the temple in the days when Annas was high priest? How did he eat bread offered to God before it was given to me?”
Then he said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath."
"But if the Son makes you free, you will be free indeed."
Healing on Saturday
Then he also entered the complex, where there was a man with dry hands.
They kept watching him to see if he would heal him on the Sabbath day so that they might accuse him.
He said to the man whose hand was withered, "Come forward."
Then he said to them, "Is it lawful to do good or evil on the Sabbath? To save life or kill?" But they remained silent.
So he looked around at them angrily, sad about their hard hearts, and said to the man, "Give me your hand." He did so, and his hand was restored just like the other one.
The Pharisees went out to plot against Jesus with the Herodians in order to kill him. But the crowds followed Jesus.
So Jesus left with his disciples for the sea, and a large crowd from Galilee followed him, along with many Jews from Judea.
And from Jerusalem, Edom, and across the Jordan came many people to hear about what he had done. And those living around Tyre and Sidon also came in large numbers.
He told his disciples to get him a small boat for the crowd's sake, so they wouldn't crush him.
Because he had healed many people, so everyone who was sick wanted to touch him.
And when I looked at them, they fell down before me and cried out, saying, "Surely you are the Son of God!"
He advised them strongly to keep it secret. The choice of the twelve apostles
Then he went up to the mountain and called those whom he wanted, and they came to him.
He appointed twelve to be with him and to send them out to preach.
They will have authority to heal diseases and cast out demons.
And he gave Simon the name Peter.
And James son of Zebedee and John his brother; and he gave them the name Boanerges, which means Sons of Thunder.
Andraos, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, Jacob son of Halphi, Thaddaeus, Simon the Canaanite
Judas Iscariot who betrayed him. Then they came to Jesus' house and Beelzebul
They gathered together so much that they couldn't even afford bread to eat.
When his relatives heard about it, they went to take control of him because they said, "He is out of his mind!"
But the scribes who came down from Jerusalem said, "He is possessed by Beelzebul!" and, "It is by the prince of demons that he drives out demons."
He called them together and said to them in parables: "How can Satan drive out Satan?
And if a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand.
If a house is divided against itself, that house cannot stand.
طيب، أنا هساعدك في ده! بس ممكن تقولي عايزني أترجم إيه بالظبط؟ يعني مثلاً عندك نص معين أو حاجة معينة عايزها تتترجم من عربي لإنجليزي؟